### COST-2: Sequential Tool Execution — Missed Parallelism

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Rule ID** | COST-2 |
| **Severity** | Medium |
| **Location** | Lines 6–9 (`for...of` loop with `await executeTool`) |

**Description:**
When `response.toolCalls` contains multiple tool calls, they are executed sequentially via the `for...of` loop with `await` on line 7. Each tool call must complete before the next begins. If tool calls are independent (which is the common case for parallel tool use in LLM APIs — e.g., OpenAI's parallel function calling), this unnecessarily serializes latency.

For $n$ tool calls each taking $d$ seconds, the current code takes $O(n \cdot d)$ wall-clock time, while parallel execution would take $O(d)$. During this serial wait time, the compute resources (Lambda, container, VM) hosting this function are idle but billed.

**Recommendation:**
Execute independent tool calls concurrently with `Promise.all`:

```typescript
if (response.toolCalls) {
  const results = await Promise.all(
    response.toolCalls.map(call => executeTool(call.name, call.args))
  );
  for (let i = 0; i < response.toolCalls.length; i++) {
    messages.push({ role: "tool", content: JSON.stringify(results[i]) });
  }
}
```

**Estimated impact:** If an average loop iteration has 3 tool calls at ~500ms each, this reduces per-iteration wall time from ~1.5s to ~0.5s, cutting compute-time costs by up to ~66% per iteration and improving end-user latency proportionally.

